// Heddle authorization rule pack shared by Weft and Worker WASM.
//
// Loaded into every Authorizer via `code()`. Tokens carry concrete `spool`
// rights; parent-to-descendant inheritance is walked after fact extraction by
// `BiscuitFacts::has_action_through_inheritance`.
//
// What's left here is the action hierarchy on a single resource,
// where every variable in the head is bound by the body fact.
//
// Current convention: facts use `right($kind, $path, $action)`.
// Constructors also emit thread/context kinds and merge/approve actions;
// the hierarchy immediately below applies only to read/write/admin on the
// same kind and path.
//
// `admin()` is the global-admin marker. It's NOT used to derive
// concrete `right(...)` facts (that would leave $k/$p/$a unbound at
// mint time too); instead, [`BiscuitFacts::is_admin`] checks the
// fact directly and short-circuits per-resource permission checks.
//
// SECURITY / E2 CUTOVER GATE (weft#248):
// `grant_envelope_conformance_tests` fuzzes this action lattice together with
// exact resource/path subset enforcement. The Postgres-backed companion in
// `weft-server/tests/grant_envelope_materialization_integration.rs` covers the
// hosted-grant graph materialized at mint. `MIN_BROWSER_SESSION_ISSUED_AT`
// must not flip while either half is absent or failing.

// Action hierarchy: admin implies write implies read.
// All three head variables ($k, $p) are bound by the body predicate,
// so the parser accepts these rules.
right($k, $p, "read")  <- right($k, $p, "write");
right($k, $p, "read")  <- right($k, $p, "admin");
right($k, $p, "write") <- right($k, $p, "admin");
